<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!--
  ======================================================================
  Platform reactor.

  Two modules, built in dependency order by one command:

      domainrules   the Sprint 5 domain. Plain Java, jakarta.validation
                    and nothing else. Produces com.yellow:sprint5-domain.

      trade-api     the Sprint 6 Trade REST API. Spring Boot, MyBatis,
                    Postgres. Declares a dependency on the jar above.

  WHY A REACTOR RATHER THAN TWO SEPARATE BUILDS.

  The domain is an independent package with its own jar and its own
  coordinates, and trade-api consumes it as a dependency, which is the
  normal way to share code and keeps exactly one copy of the rules.

  Doing that with `mvn install` in one folder and then `mvn install` in
  the other works, but it puts the dependency in ~/.m2: a directory
  outside the repository, per-machine, and invisible to git. A fresh
  clone would not build, and an image build starts with an empty ~/.m2
  and cannot reach anybody's laptop.

  A reactor removes that coupling without removing the boundary. Maven
  resolves sprint5-domain from the build itself, so:

      mvn clean install

  from this directory builds both, in order, on a machine that has never
  seen the code. Criterion 1 holds and the module boundary is real.

  WHAT THE BOUNDARY BUYS, concretely: domainrules keeps its own build,
  so the enforcer plugin's bannedDependencies rule still fails the build
  the moment Spring, a servlet API, MyBatis, a JDBC driver or a
  connection pool reaches the domain's classpath. That guarantee is
  mechanical here. It cannot be, if the domain is a folder of source
  inside a Spring Boot project where all of those legitimately resolve.

  NOTE ON PARENTS. This pom aggregates; it does not parent. trade-api
  keeps spring-boot-starter-parent as its parent for dependency
  management, and domainrules keeps no parent at all so that nothing
  Spring-shaped can arrive by inheritance. In Maven, aggregation and
  inheritance are independent, and here they are deliberately different.
  ======================================================================
-->
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0
         https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.yellow</groupId>
    <artifactId>fauxnance-platform</artifactId>
    <version>1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>

    <name>Fauxnance Trading Platform</name>
    <description>
        Reactor for the Sprint 5 domain engine and the Sprint 6 Trade REST API.
    </description>

    <!-- Order matters only for readability: Maven sorts by dependency. -->
    <modules>
        <module>domainrules</module>
        <module>trade-api</module>
    </modules>

</project>
